  <!-- Deep Scientific Principles (3 Cards) -->
  <div class="border-t border-hairline pt-10 mb-10">
    <div class="mb-6">
      <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-bold text-ink tracking-tight mb-1">
        {d.scienceTitle}
      </h3>
      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-muted">
        {d.scienceSub}
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6 text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed">
      <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-surface-soft border border-hairline space-y-2">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">
          <span class="text-emerald-600 font-mono">1.</span> Bigelow General Thermal Lethality (B_v)
        </h4>
        <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 text-emerald-300 font-mono text-[11px] my-2">
          B_v = &int; 10^[(T(t) - 100&deg;C) / z] dt
        </div>
        <p>
          Continuous vegetable blanching integrates thermal lethal rate over transit dwell time <code class="font-mono text-ink">t</code>. For blanching quality kinetics, reference temperature is standardized to <code class="font-mono text-ink">100&deg;C (212&deg;F)</code> with typical enzyme inactivation z-value <code class="font-mono text-ink">z = 10.0&deg;C</code>. Achieving <code class="font-mono text-ink">B_v &ge; 1.5 min</code> guarantees core temperature penetration exceeding 88°C, inactivating thermal-resistant native peroxidases.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-surface-soft border border-hairline space-y-2">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">
          <span class="text-emerald-600 font-mono">2.</span> Geobacillus stearothermophilus D-Values
        </h4>
        <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 text-emerald-300 font-mono text-[11px] my-2">
          log(N_t / N_0) = - t / D_T = - t / [D_ref &middot; 10^((121.1 - T) / z)]
        </div>
        <p>
          <code class="font-mono text-ink">G. stearothermophilus</code> endospores represent the gold standard thermophile in low-acid canning (<code class="font-mono text-ink">D_121.1 = 3.5 to 4.5 min, z = 10&deg;C</code>). At atmospheric blanching temperatures (90°C–98°C), spores survive partially (<code class="font-mono text-ink">D_100 &approx; 85 min</code>). If cold stratification drops drum zones below 85°C, spore reduction collapses, allowing vegetative outgrowth and flat-sour spoilage under warm distribution.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-surface-soft border border-hairline space-y-2">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">
          <span class="text-emerald-600 font-mono">3.</span> Auger Retention Time & Axial Dispersion
        </h4>
        <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 text-emerald-300 font-mono text-[11px] my-2">
          Pe = u &middot; L / D_ax &nbsp;&ge;&nbsp; 80 (Plug-Flow Hydrodynamics)
        </div>
        <p>
          Helical auger flights convey product forward as a progressing plug flow. If rotational RPM is too high or flights suffer excessive clearances (&gt;3mm gap), vegetable kernels short-circuit through flight clearances, resulting in non-uniform retention times (<code class="font-mono text-ink">Pe &lt; 30</code>) where up to 8% of kernels exit under-processed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Comparative Technical Systems Table -->
  <div class="border-t border-hairline pt-10 mb-10">
    <div class="mb-6">
